    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'll check the saw. i've also been thinking about the grain direction lately too. sometimes it feels like even when i'm cutting with the grain, the fibers just act up and tear right at the end of the stroke. is there a specific way i should be managing the wood fibers or the moisture to prevent that? maybe a certain way to approach the chisel work?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When dealing with wood fibers and moisture, make sure the wood is completely dry. Even when cutting with the grain, excessive moisture or tension can cause fibers to tear. Clamp the wood flat and secure, and use a sharp chisel and mallet with gentle tapping. To alleviate fibers acting up, place a scrap piece of wood beneath the cut end to support and catch any fibers. Grip the chisel firmly and cut straight without applying too much pressure. Reducing the speed of your cut may also help manage the fibers more effectively. Vors caution with the angle of the chisel; keep it as perpendicular as possible to the grain to minimize tearing. Lastly, frequently check for dullness and sharpen the edges of your tools regularly.",
