    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, i'll definitely try the support scrap underneath. do you think the type of chisel matters a lot here? i've been using a standard bevel edge but i've heard some people say a different grind helps with fine detail. also, is there anything else that might cause such unpredictable splintering right at the very end? maybe something about the wood itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The type of chisel can indeed make a difference, especially for delicate work such as dovetails. A chisel with a paring or half-round bevel edge offers a finer, more controlled cut, reducing the risk of splintering, particularly at such critical points in the joint. The finer edge can make cleaner, closer shave to the end grain and could provide a more predictable result.\n\nAs for the wood itself, variability in moisture content, knots, or internal stresses can cause splintering. Be aware of the growth rings and grain direction, especially if you're working near potential weaknesses formed during the tree's growth. Avoid cuts near heavy knots or where the grain curves may meet, as these are more prone to splintering. For added control, use the 'outside cut' technique for fine dovetail angles, leaving the grain unexposed at its widest arc in the cut, rather than going straight through the grain, which can offer better integrity for your joints. Keeping your tools sharp and working in a clean, stable environment will further minimize unpredictable splintering.",
      "source": "target",
